            <ArticleTitle>Diagonal earlobe creases and atheromatous disease: a postmortem study.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>274-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The association between the grade of earlobe creases and degree of atheromatous disease was investigated in 376 postmortem examinations. Each earlobe crease was individually graded from 0 to 3. The risk of atherosclerosis as a cause of death was nearly doubled in men with grade 3 creases in both earlobes, and more than trebled in women with bilateral high-grade creases. The risk of death from myocardial infarction was 2.50 in men with high-grade creases and 3.70 in women. The earlobe crease is an important sign of atheromatous disease and it may be useful to include it in the clinical examination of patients.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
